IMPORT prelude

-- Restraining Orders Act 1997 (Western Australia) -- which restraining order can protect me: the family violence restraining
-- order (FVRO, Part 1B), the violence restraining order (VRO, Part 2) and the misconduct restraining order (MRO, Part 3);
-- who may apply; telephone applications and hearings in the absence of the respondent (interim orders); police orders
-- (Part 2A Div 3A); how long each order lasts; and the offence of breaching an order (ss 61, 61A, 61B).
--
-- SEAT: the person threatened -- the person seeking to be protected. They ask "What order can protect me?", "Can I apply
-- myself?", "Can I get an order before the respondent is heard?", "Can the police act now?", "How long would it protect
-- me?" and "What happens to the respondent if they breach it?". The Act addresses the court, the police and the person
-- bound; each such provision is encoded as what it means for the person protected. Where the Act hands a judgment to the
-- court or a police officer (whether the respondent "is likely" to do it again, whether an apprehension is on "reasonable
-- grounds", whether an order is "appropriate", whether there are "special circumstances", whether a matter is urgent),
-- the answer is conditional on that judgment, which is asked as a fact.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 3 (MRO, FVRO, VRO, police order, interim order, satisfied), s 4 (family relationship), s 5A(1)
-- (family violence), s 6(2) (personal violence), s 7 (natural persons), s 10D (when FVROs may be made), s 11A, s 12A (when
-- VROs may be made; not in a family relationship), ss 16(4)(d), 16A(2)(a), 16B(a) (duration of FVRO and VRO), s 18 and
-- s 20(1) (telephone applications), s 24A(1), (2)(a), (b) (who may apply for an FVRO), s 25(1), (2) (VRO), s 26 and
-- s 29(1)(a) (first hearing in the absence of the respondent), s 30A(1)-(3) (police orders), s 30F (their duration),
-- s 31 (21 days to object), s 34 (grounds for an MRO, paras (a)(i)-(iii) and (b)), s 35A, s 37 (duration of MRO), s 38(1),
-- (2) (who may apply for an MRO), s 39, s 50, s 50A (orders against children), s 61(1), (1A), (2), (2a) (breach),
-- s 61A(2), (2B), (5), (6) (repeated breach), s 61B(3) (the protected person who aids a breach).
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (law 1817, mrdoc 48927, retrieved 7 September 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_48927.txt. No regulations set a number used here: every number ($10 000, $1 000,
-- 2 years, 1 year, 72 hours, 24 hours, 3 months, 21 days, 6 months, the ages 10, 16 and 18) is the Act's own.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: FVROs for a child exposed to family violence (s 10E); the matters the court considers (ss 10F, 12, 35,
-- asked only through the court's ultimate satisfaction); the terms of the restraints themselves (ss 10G, 13, 36, 30C,
-- listed in comments only); firearms and explosives orders (ss 14, 14A); behaviour management orders (Part 1C); conduct
-- agreements and consent orders (ss 10H, 41) beyond noting them; the MRO ground in s 34(a)(iv) (Criminal Code s 70A(2A),
-- animal source food production); the respondent's procedure after an interim order beyond the 21 days (ss 32, 33);
-- prisoners (s 16A(3)); orders in other proceedings and on conviction (ss 63, 63A); variation and cancellation (Part 5);
-- children's evidence and special conferences (Parts 4, 5A); service (Part 6) beyond the police order rules; the s 62
-- defences; police powers of entry and search (ss 62A-62G); interstate and foreign orders (Parts 7, 7A); and the machinery.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Restraining Orders Act 1997 (WA)`

§§ `numbers`

-- s 50: "No restraining order is to be made against a child who is under 10 years of age."
`the youngest respondent` MEANS 10
-- s 3: "child means a person under 18 years of age".
`the age of adulthood` MEANS 18
-- s 24A(1)(a): the person seeking to be protected may apply for an FVRO "if the person is 16 years of age or older".
`the youngest FVRO applicant` MEANS 16
-- s 16A(2)(a)(ii), s 16B(a)(ii): "if no period is specified, 2 years".
`FVRO or VRO default months` MEANS 24
-- s 37(2): "if no period is specified, for one year".
`MRO default months` MEANS 12
-- s 50A: a final order against a child "is to have a duration of 6 months or less".
`child respondent cap months` MEANS 6

§§ `the order and the applicant`

-- s 3: "restraining order means an FVRO, MRO or VRO"; "police order means an order made by a police officer under Part 2A
-- Division 3A".
DECLARE `Kind of order` IS ONE OF
    `an FVRO`          -- family violence restraining order (Part 1B)
    `a VRO`            -- violence restraining order (Part 2)
    `an MRO`           -- misconduct restraining order (Part 3)
    `a police order`   -- made on the spot by a police officer (Part 2A Div 3A); not a restraining order

-- ss 24A, 25, 38: who may apply.
DECLARE `Applicant` IS ONE OF
    `me`
    `a police officer for me`
    `my parent or guardian, because I am a child`
    `a child welfare officer, because I am a child`
    `my guardian under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990`

§§ `4 -- family relationship`

-- s 4(1): "family relationship means a relationship between 2 persons -- (a) who are, or were, married to each other; or
-- (b) who are, or were, in a de facto relationship with each other; or (c) who are, or were, related to each other; or
-- (d) one of whom is a child who ... ordinarily resides, or resided, with the other person; or ... regularly resides or
-- stays ...; or (e) one of whom is, or was, a child of whom the other person is a guardian; or (f) who have, or had, an
-- intimate personal relationship, or other personal relationship, with each other; or (g) one of whom is the former spouse
-- or former de facto partner of the other person's current spouse or current de facto partner." "Related" (s 4(2)) takes
-- in cultural, social or religious background and in-laws; it is asked as one fact.
DECLARE `The relationship between us` HAS
    `we are or were married`                                              IS A BOOLEAN  -- (a)
    `we are or were in a de facto relationship`                           IS A BOOLEAN  -- (b)
    `we are or were related`                                              IS A BOOLEAN  -- (c), with s 4(2)
    `one of us is a child who lives or stays, or did, with the other`     IS A BOOLEAN  -- (d)
    `one of us is or was a child of whom the other is a guardian`         IS A BOOLEAN  -- (e)
    `we have or had an intimate or other personal relationship`           IS A BOOLEAN  -- (f), with s 4(2)
    `one of us is the former partner of the other's current partner`      IS A BOOLEAN  -- (g)

@export Are the respondent and I in a family relationship (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 4)?
GIVEN `r` IS A `The relationship between us` @desc How you and the respondent are, or were, connected
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are we in a family relationship` IF
       `r`'s `we are or were married`
    OR `r`'s `we are or were in a de facto relationship`
    OR `r`'s `we are or were related`
    OR `r`'s `one of us is a child who lives or stays, or did, with the other`
    OR `r`'s `one of us is or was a child of whom the other is a guardian`
    OR `r`'s `we have or had an intimate or other personal relationship`
    OR `r`'s `one of us is the former partner of the other's current partner`

§§ `5A, 6 -- family violence and personal violence`

-- s 5A(1): "family violence is a reference to -- (a) violence, or a threat of violence, by a person towards a family member
-- of the person; or (b) any other behaviour or pattern of behaviour by the person that coerces or controls the family
-- member or causes the member to be fearful." s 5A(2)(e) gives "damaging or destroying property of the family member" as
-- an example.
-- s 6(2): "personal violence means one of the following acts that a person commits against another person with whom he or
-- she is not in a family relationship -- (a) assaulting or causing personal injury to the person; (b) kidnapping, or
-- depriving the liberty of, the person; (c) stalking the person; (d) threatening to commit any act described in paragraph
-- (a) or (b) against the person; (e) if the person who commits the act has an imagined personal relationship with the
-- person ..., an act that would constitute family violence if those persons were in a family relationship."
-- s 3: "imagined personal relationship means a relationship where one person claims to have, or have had, an intimate
-- personal relationship, or other personal relationship, with another person but that other person disagrees".
DECLARE `What the respondent has done to me` HAS
    `used or threatened violence towards me`                              IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 5A(1)(a)
    `behaved in a way that coerces or controls me or makes me fearful`    IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 5A(1)(b), incl. property damage
    `assaulted or injured me`                                             IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 6(2)(a)
    `kidnapped me or deprived me of my liberty`                           IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 6(2)(b)
    `stalked me`                                                          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 6(2)(c)
    `threatened to assault, injure or kidnap me`                          IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 6(2)(d)
    `claims a personal relationship with me that I deny`                  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 6(2)(e), s 3

@export Has the respondent committed family violence against me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 5A)?
GIVEN `r` IS A `The relationship between us` @desc How you and the respondent are, or were, connected
      `d` IS A `What the respondent has done to me` @desc What the respondent has done or threatened
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the respondent committed family violence against me` IF
        `Are we in a family relationship` `r`
    AND (    `d`'s `used or threatened violence towards me`
          OR `d`'s `behaved in a way that coerces or controls me or makes me fearful`)

@export Has the respondent committed personal violence against me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 6)?
GIVEN `r` IS A `The relationship between us` @desc How you and the respondent are, or were, connected
      `d` IS A `What the respondent has done to me` @desc What the respondent has done or threatened
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the respondent committed personal violence against me` IF
        (NOT (`Are we in a family relationship` `r`))
    AND (    `d`'s `assaulted or injured me`
          OR `d`'s `kidnapped me or deprived me of my liberty`
          OR `d`'s `stalked me`
          OR `d`'s `threatened to assault, injure or kidnap me`
          OR (    `d`'s `claims a personal relationship with me that I deny`
              AND (    `d`'s `used or threatened violence towards me`
                    OR `d`'s `behaved in a way that coerces or controls me or makes me fearful`)))

§§ `10D, 11A, 12A, 34, 35A, 7, 50 -- which order can protect me`

-- The court's judgments, each asked as a fact. s 3: "satisfied means satisfied on the balance of probabilities".
-- s 10D(1): "A court may make an FVRO if it is satisfied that -- (a) the respondent has committed family violence against a
-- person seeking to be protected and the respondent is likely again to commit family violence against that person in the
-- future; or (b) a person seeking to be protected ... has reasonable grounds to apprehend that the respondent will commit
-- family violence against the person seeking to be protected." (2): "the court must make the order unless there are
-- special circumstances that would make the order inappropriate."
-- s 11A: a VRO, on the same two limbs for personal violence, "and that making a VRO is appropriate in the circumstances".
-- s 34: "A court may make an MRO if it is satisfied that -- (a) unless restrained, the respondent is likely to -- (i)
-- behave in a manner that could reasonably be expected to be intimidating or offensive to the person seeking to be
-- protected and that would, in fact, intimidate or offend the person seeking to be protected; or (ii) cause damage to
-- property owned by, or in the possession of, the person seeking to be protected; or (iii) behave in a manner that is, or
-- is likely to lead to, a breach of the peace; ... and (b) granting an MRO is appropriate in the circumstances."
DECLARE `What the court is satisfied of` HAS
    `the respondent is likely to commit that violence against me again`                  IS A BOOLEAN  -- ss 10D(1)(a), 11A(a)
    `I have reasonable grounds to apprehend the respondent will commit that violence`    IS A BOOLEAN  -- ss 10D(1)(b), 11A(b)
    `special circumstances make an FVRO inappropriate`                                   IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 10D(2)
    `an order is appropriate in the circumstances`                                       IS A BOOLEAN  -- ss 11A, 34(b)
    `unless restrained the respondent is likely to intimidate or offend me`              IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 34(a)(i)
    `unless restrained the respondent is likely to damage my property`                   IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 34(a)(ii)
    `unless restrained the respondent is likely to breach the peace`                     IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 34(a)(iii)

-- One case: one person seeking to be protected, one respondent.
DECLARE `My case` HAS
    `the relationship`                        IS A `The relationship between us`
    `what the respondent did`                 IS A `What the respondent has done to me`
    `the court`                               IS A `What the court is satisfied of`
    `the respondent is an individual`         IS A BOOLEAN   -- s 7: "must be natural persons"; not a company
    `the respondent's age in years`           IS A NUMBER    -- s 50

-- s 7: "a person who is bound by an order under this Act, must be natural persons"; s 50: none against a child under 10.
@export Can any restraining order be made against this respondent at all (Restraining Orders Act 1997 ss 7, 50)?
GIVEN `c` IS A `My case` @desc Your relationship to the respondent, what they did, what the court is satisfied of, and who they are
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can an order be made against this respondent` IF
    `c`'s `the respondent is an individual` AND `c`'s `the respondent's age in years` AT LEAST `the youngest respondent`

@export Can a court make a family violence restraining order for me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 10D(1))?
GIVEN `c` IS A `My case` @desc Your relationship to the respondent, what they did, what the court is satisfied of, and who they are
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can a court make an FVRO for me` IF
        `Can an order be made against this respondent` `c`
    AND `Are we in a family relationship` (`c`'s `the relationship`)
    AND (    (    `Has the respondent committed family violence against me` (`c`'s `the relationship`) (`c`'s `what the respondent did`)
              AND `c`'s `the court`'s `the respondent is likely to commit that violence against me again`)
          OR `c`'s `the court`'s `I have reasonable grounds to apprehend the respondent will commit that violence`)

@export Must the court make the family violence restraining order (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 10D(2))?
GIVEN `c` IS A `My case` @desc Your relationship to the respondent, what they did, what the court is satisfied of, and who they are
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the court make the FVRO` IF
    `Can a court make an FVRO for me` `c` AND (NOT (`c`'s `the court`'s `special circumstances make an FVRO inappropriate`))

-- s 12A: "A court is not to make a VRO unless it is satisfied that the person seeking to be protected by the order and the
-- person bound by the order are not in a family relationship with each other." s 6(2) already confines personal
-- violence to people not in a family relationship.
@export Can a court make a violence restraining order for me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 ss 11A, 12A)?
GIVEN `c` IS A `My case` @desc Your relationship to the respondent, what they did, what the court is satisfied of, and who they are
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can a court make a VRO for me` IF
        `Can an order be made against this respondent` `c`
    AND (NOT (`Are we in a family relationship` (`c`'s `the relationship`)))
    AND (    (    `Has the respondent committed personal violence against me` (`c`'s `the relationship`) (`c`'s `what the respondent did`)
              AND `c`'s `the court`'s `the respondent is likely to commit that violence against me again`)
          OR `c`'s `the court`'s `I have reasonable grounds to apprehend the respondent will commit that violence`)
    AND `c`'s `the court`'s `an order is appropriate in the circumstances`

-- s 35A: "A court is not to make an MRO unless it is satisfied that the person seeking to be protected by the order and the
-- person bound by the order are not in a family relationship with each other." s 34 looks only forward: no past conduct is
-- required.
@export Can a court make a misconduct restraining order for me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 ss 34, 35A)?
GIVEN `c` IS A `My case` @desc Your relationship to the respondent, what they did, what the court is satisfied of, and who they are
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can a court make an MRO for me` IF
        `Can an order be made against this respondent` `c`
    AND (NOT (`Are we in a family relationship` (`c`'s `the relationship`)))
    AND (    `c`'s `the court`'s `unless restrained the respondent is likely to intimidate or offend me`
          OR `c`'s `the court`'s `unless restrained the respondent is likely to damage my property`
          OR `c`'s `the court`'s `unless restrained the respondent is likely to breach the peace`)
    AND `c`'s `the court`'s `an order is appropriate in the circumstances`

DECLARE `Order that can protect me` IS ONE OF
    `a family violence restraining order`
    `a violence restraining order`
    `a misconduct restraining order`
    `none: the respondent is not an individual or is under 10`
    `none on what the court is satisfied of`

-- The family relationship sorts the Parts: within one, only an FVRO (ss 12A, 35A bar the others); outside one, a VRO or an
-- MRO. Where both a VRO and an MRO are open, the VRO is named, as the heavier order (2 years by default, and its breach
-- carries imprisonment); an applicant may still choose to seek an MRO.
@export Which restraining order can protect me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 Parts 1B, 2 and 3)?
GIVEN `c` IS A `My case` @desc Your relationship to the respondent, what they did, what the court is satisfied of, and who they are
GIVETH AN `Order that can protect me`
DECIDE `Which restraining order can protect me` IS
    IF NOT (`Can an order be made against this respondent` `c`)
    THEN `none: the respondent is not an individual or is under 10`
    ELSE IF `Can a court make an FVRO for me` `c`
         THEN `a family violence restraining order`
         ELSE IF `Can a court make a VRO for me` `c`
              THEN `a violence restraining order`
              ELSE IF `Can a court make an MRO for me` `c`
                   THEN `a misconduct restraining order`
                   ELSE `none on what the court is satisfied of`

§§ `24A, 25, 38 -- who may apply`

-- s 24A(1): an FVRO application may be made by "(a) the person seeking to be protected, if the person is 16 years of age
-- or older; or (b) a police officer on behalf of the person seeking to be protected, regardless of the age of the person."
-- (2): "(a) if the person seeking to be protected is a child, by a parent or guardian of the child, or a child welfare
-- officer, on behalf of the child; or (b) if the person seeking to be protected is a person for whom a guardian has been
-- appointed under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990, by the guardian". ss 25 and 38 are the same for a VRO and
-- an MRO, without the age limit on applying oneself. A police order is not applied for (s 30A(2)).
@export May this person apply for the order to protect me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 ss 24A, 25, 38)?
GIVEN `the order` IS A `Kind of order` @desc The order you want
      `the applicant` IS AN `Applicant` @desc Who would make the application
      `my age in years` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age
      `I have a guardian under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a guardian has been appointed for you under that Act
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May this person apply for me` IF
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `a police order` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `the applicant`
        WHEN `me` THEN
            CONSIDER `the order`
            WHEN `an FVRO` THEN `my age in years` AT LEAST `the youngest FVRO applicant`
            OTHERWISE TRUE
        WHEN `a police officer for me` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `my parent or guardian, because I am a child` THEN `my age in years` LESS THAN `the age of adulthood`
        WHEN `a child welfare officer, because I am a child` THEN `my age in years` LESS THAN `the age of adulthood`
        WHEN `my guardian under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990` THEN `I have a guardian under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990`

§§ `18, 20, 26, 29, 39 -- interim orders`

-- s 3: "telephone application means an application under Division 2 for an FVRO or VRO". s 18(1): by "an authorised person
-- on behalf of the person seeking to be protected; or ... the person seeking to be protected if he or she is introduced to
-- the authorised magistrate by an authorised person" (an authorised person is a police officer or a prescribed person).
-- s 20(1): heard if the magistrate is satisfied that applying in person "would not be practical" because of the time,
-- place or urgency, or "some other factor ... justifies making an FVRO or VRO as a matter of urgency".
@export Can I get a restraining order by telephone, without going to court (Restraining Orders Act 1997 ss 18, 20)?
GIVEN `the order` IS A `Kind of order` @desc The order you want
      `an authorised person applies for me or introduces me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a police officer (or other authorised person) makes the application or introduces you to the magistrate
      `the magistrate is satisfied it is urgent or impractical to apply in person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the magistrate accepts that going to court in person is impractical or the order is urgent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I get a telephone order` IF
        (CONSIDER `the order`
         WHEN `an FVRO` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `a VRO` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE)
    AND `an authorised person applies for me or introduces me`
    AND `the magistrate is satisfied it is urgent or impractical to apply in person`

-- s 26(1): in an application for an FVRO or VRO "the applicant must indicate whether the applicant wishes to -- (a) have
-- the first hearing of the application held in the absence of the respondent; or (b) proceed directly to a defended
-- hearing." s 29(1)(a): at that hearing the court may "make an FVRO or VRO"; over 72 hours it is an interim order (s 29(3)).
-- s 39: for an MRO "the registrar is to fix a hearing and summons the respondent to the hearing": there is no hearing in
-- the respondent's absence by choice, and no interim MRO.
@export Can I ask for a first hearing without the respondent, to get an interim order (Restraining Orders Act 1997 ss 26, 29, 39)?
GIVEN `the order` IS A `Kind of order` @desc The order you want
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I get an interim order before the respondent is heard` IF
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `an FVRO` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a VRO` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 31: "Within 21 days of being served with an interim order a respondent must ... return" the endorsement copy; if not
-- returned, the interim order becomes final at the end of the 21 days (s 32(2), (3)(b)).
@export How many days does the respondent have to object to my interim order (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 31)?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days does the respondent have to object` IS 21

-- s 16(4)(d): an interim telephone order ends, at the latest, when "3 months elapse from the time the order came into force".
@export At most how many months does an interim telephone order protect me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 16(4)(d))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many months can a telephone order protect me at most` IS 3

§§ `30A, 30F -- police orders`

-- s 30A(1): "A police officer may make a police order ... if the officer reasonably believes that the case meets the
-- criteria set out in section 20(1)(a) or (b) as if the order were to be an FVRO and -- (a) if the officer reasonably
-- believes that -- (i) a person has committed family violence and is likely again to commit that violence; ... or (b) if
-- the officer has reasonable grounds to apprehend ... that -- (i) a person will have committed against the person family
-- violence; ... and that making a police order is necessary to ensure the safety of a person." Read: the necessity words
-- sit inside paragraph (b), so they govern only (b). (3): not "if a telephone application has been dismissed under
-- section 23(1)(b) in relation to the same facts." Family violence needs a family relationship (s 5A), so police orders
-- are only for the family.
@export Can a police officer make an order on the spot to protect me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 30A)?
GIVEN `r` IS A `The relationship between us` @desc How you and the respondent are, or were, connected
      `the officer believes it is urgent or impractical to go to court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the officer reasonably believes the s 20(1) urgency criteria are met
      `the officer believes family violence was committed and is likely again` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the officer reasonably believes the respondent committed family violence and will do it again
      `the officer has reasonable grounds to apprehend family violence against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the officer has reasonable grounds to apprehend family violence will be committed against you
      `the officer believes an order is necessary for someone's safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the officer believes an order is necessary to ensure someone's safety
      `a telephone application on the same facts was dismissed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a magistrate dismissed a telephone application about the same facts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the police make an order to protect me now` IF
        `Are we in a family relationship` `r`
    AND `the officer believes it is urgent or impractical to go to court`
    AND (    `the officer believes family violence was committed and is likely again`
          OR (    `the officer has reasonable grounds to apprehend family violence against me`
              AND `the officer believes an order is necessary for someone's safety`))
    AND (NOT `a telephone application on the same facts was dismissed`)

-- s 30F(1): a police order "(a) remains in force for 72 hours (or any shorter period specified ...) after it has been
-- served ...; and (b) lapses if it is not served ... within 24 hours of the order being made." s 30H: it "cannot be
-- extended or renewed".
@export At most how many hours does a police order protect me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 30F)?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many hours can a police order protect me` IS 72

@export Within how many hours must a police order be served, or it lapses (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 30F(1)(b))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `Within how many hours must a police order be served` IS 24

§§ `16A, 16B, 37, 50A -- how long a final order lasts`

-- s 16A(2)(a), s 16B(a): an FVRO or VRO made at a final order hearing remains in force for "the period (of whatever
-- duration) specified in the order ...; or ... if no period is specified, 2 years". s 37(2): an MRO "remains in force for
-- the period specified in the order or, if no period is specified, for one year". s 50A: "A restraining order that is a
-- final order made against a child is to have a duration of 6 months or less, unless the order is made under section 63A."
-- A police order is not a final order; it lasts hours (s 30F), so the answer is 0 months. Respondents in prison
-- (s 16A(3)) and s 63A orders are not encoded.
@export How many months would the final order protect me (Restraining Orders Act 1997 ss 16A, 16B, 37, 50A)?
GIVEN `the order` IS A `Kind of order` @desc The order made
      `months specified in the order` IS A NUMBER @desc The period the order specifies, in months; 0 if it specifies none
      `the respondent is a child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent is under 18
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many months would the final order protect me` IS
    IF `the respondent is a child` AND `the length before the child cap` AT LEAST `child respondent cap months`
    THEN `child respondent cap months`
    ELSE `the length before the child cap`
    WHERE
        `the length before the child cap` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the order`
            WHEN `a police order` THEN 0
            WHEN `an MRO` THEN
                IF `months specified in the order` GREATER THAN 0 THEN `months specified in the order` ELSE `MRO default months`
            OTHERWISE
                IF `months specified in the order` GREATER THAN 0 THEN `months specified in the order` ELSE `FVRO or VRO default months`

§§ `61, 61A, 61B -- breach`

-- s 61: "(1) A person who is bound by an FVRO and who breaches that order commits an offence. Penalty ...: a fine of
-- $10 000 or imprisonment for 2 years, or both. (1A) [VRO, the same] (2) A person who is bound by an MRO and who breaches
-- that order commits an offence. Penalty ...: a fine of $1 000. (2a) [police order, as for an FVRO]".
@export What is the most the respondent can be fined for breaching my order, in dollars (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 61)?
GIVEN `the order` IS A `Kind of order` @desc The order breached
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most the respondent can be fined for a breach` IS
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `an MRO` THEN 1000
    OTHERWISE 10000

@export What is the longest the respondent can be imprisoned for breaching my order, in months (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 61)?
GIVEN `the order` IS A `Kind of order` @desc The order breached
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the longest imprisonment for a breach in months` IS
    CONSIDER `the order`
    WHEN `an MRO` THEN 0
    OTHERWISE 24

-- s 61A(1): a "relevant offence" is an offence under s 61(1), (1A) or (2a) (or aggravated stalking under The Criminal Code
-- s 338E, not encoded) -- not an MRO breach. (2): it applies if the person is convicted of a relevant offence and "has
-- committed, and been convicted of, at least 2 offences that are also relevant offences ... within the period of 2 years
-- before" that conviction. (2B): previous offences "committed on the same day are to be treated as a single conviction".
-- (5): an adult "must" receive a penalty that "is or includes imprisonment" (for a child, (4): imprisonment or detention),
-- unless (6) "(a) imprisonment or detention would be clearly unjust ...; and (b) the person is unlikely to be a threat to
-- the safety of a person protected or the community generally" -- the court's judgment, asked as a fact.
@export Must the respondent be locked up for breaching my order again (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 61A)?
GIVEN `the order` IS A `Kind of order` @desc The order breached this time
      `days with earlier breach convictions in the 2 years before` IS A NUMBER @desc On how many different days, in the 2 years before this conviction, the respondent committed FVRO, VRO or police order breaches they were convicted of
      `the court finds the s 61A(6) exception` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds imprisonment clearly unjust and the respondent unlikely to threaten anyone's safety
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the respondent be locked up for a repeated breach` IF
        (CONSIDER `the order`
         WHEN `an MRO` THEN FALSE
         OTHERWISE TRUE)
    AND `days with earlier breach convictions in the 2 years before` AT LEAST 2
    AND (NOT `the court finds the s 61A(6) exception`)

-- s 61B(3): "Despite The Criminal Code section 7, the protected person does not commit an offence under section 61 by
-- aiding the breach of the order."
@export Do I commit an offence if I help the respondent breach my order (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 61B(3))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I commit an offence by aiding a breach of my order` IS FALSE
